{
 "id": "2020-cho-retail",
 "name": "Retail Interior Colour: Perceived Luxury and Preference to Stay",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: the 12 store renderings from Table 1 of Cho & Suh (2020), Frontiers in Psychology 11: 296 (CC BY 4.0). One hypothetical clothing store is shown with a high-luxury colour combination (dark brown, brown, ivory; stores A–F) and a low-luxury combination (brown, orange, green; stores O–T), each applied to floor, main wall and furniture, and recessed wall and seating in six different ways. For each set, as in the paper, participants view each rendering for 20 s, view all six together for 20 s, then after another 20 s of looking pick the most and least luxurious store with a reason, and after 20 s more pick the store they would most and least prefer to stay in with a reason. The page embeds which colour sits on which surface and the paper's vote counts (e.g. most luxurious: E and Q; most preferred to stay: A and P). Differences from the original study: (1) online, without eye tracking (the paper also analysed dwell time and pupil size); (2) reasons are typed instead of spoken; (3) a question about design background is added because the paper's 26 participants were interior design students; (4) the renderings are crops of the published table (about 540×290 px).",
